WEBVTT

NOTE MACHINE-GENERATED DRAFT — HUMAN QA REQUIRED. LO 17; gemini; gemini-3.1-flash-lite.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
Welcome back to Learn in Curve.

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:07.500
Today, we're jumping right into Learning
Objective 17, Solutions Development.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.065
You know that feeling when you're staring
at a massive, overwhelming list of project

4
00:00:12.065 --> 00:00:12.500
demands?

5
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.000
You're just wondering, how on earth are we
actually going to pull this off?

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.750
Well, you're in the right place.

7
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.500
Today, we're going to demystify all that
complexity.

8
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.389
We'll figure out exactly how to evaluate
and prioritize requirements to decide what

9
00:00:25.389 --> 00:00:30.219
to build, and just as importantly, how to
build it to deliver the absolute optimal

10
00:00:30.219 --> 00:00:30.750
solution.

11
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.500
Okay, let's dive right into our roadmap
for today.

12
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.000
First, we'll look at defining the core
problem.

13
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.873
Then, evaluating the available options,
the art of prioritizing, delivering the

14
00:00:41.873 --> 00:00:46.500
optimal solution, and finally, how
solutions work in different life cycles.

15
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.744
All right, part one, defining the core
problem and navigating conflicting

16
00:00:51.744 --> 00:00:52.500
requirements.

17
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.922
So, the reality of project management is,
well, it's usually pretty messy right at

18
00:00:57.922 --> 00:00:59.000
the start, isn't it?

19
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.578
Projects almost always kick off with this
crazy storm of conflicting needs, vague

20
00:01:03.578 --> 00:01:05.500
objectives, and really big dreams.

21
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.000
Let's imagine a scenario to bring this to
life.

22
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.500
Think of the snappy appy office
renovation.

23
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.752
You're the project manager, and AO, the
passionate founder, comes to you with this

24
00:01:14.752 --> 00:01:17.500
massive wish list for their perfect new
headquarters.

25
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.606
They want a quiet mindfulness space,
interactive whiteboards in literally every

26
00:01:21.606 --> 00:01:25.556
meeting room, a lush green wall in the
kitchen for well-being, and sit/stand

27
00:01:25.556 --> 00:01:28.000
adjustable desks for the customer service
team.

28
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.500
Sounds amazing, right?

29
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.500
But here's the catch.

30
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.500
The building is grade two listed.

31
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.159
That means there are incredibly strict
regulations on what you can actually

32
00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:36.500
change.

33
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.500
Suddenly, AO's dream list is crashing
headfirst into legal building constraints.

34
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.500
So, what do you do?

35
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.326
Well, this is where we really need to nail
down what solutions development actually

36
00:01:47.326 --> 00:01:47.500
is.

37
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.312
Basically, it's the ability to determine
the absolute optimal solution to satisfy

38
00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.500
agreed requirements.

39
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.500
The golden rule here?

40
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:59.372
Before we build a single thing or, you
know, knock down any walls in AO's office,

41
00:01:59.372 --> 00:02:02.500
we need crystal clear clarity on the
problem itself.

42
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.500
Have you ever heard the Russian space pen
anecdote?

43
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.500
It's famous in project management.

44
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.500
The story goes that during the space race,
millions of dollars were spent developing

45
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.500
this highly sophisticated pen that could
write in zero gravity.

46
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.500
The Russian solution?

47
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.000
They just used a pencil.

48
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.500
Now, whether you buy into the complete
historical accuracy of that or not,

49
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.000
the lesson is absolutely brilliant.

50
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.477
It perfectly highlights the trap of
jumping straight to a complex solution

51
00:02:25.477 --> 00:02:29.000
before you've actually figured out the
core problem you're trying to solve.

52
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.121
Moving right along to part two, evaluating
the available options and filtering

53
00:02:34.121 --> 00:02:35.500
through the criteria.

54
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.186
Now that we actually know the problem
we're trying to solve, we move into the

55
00:02:40.186 --> 00:02:41.000
evaluation phase.

56
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.500
Look, every project is unique, right?

57
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.000
Which means you have to look at your
options through five specific lenses.

58
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.500
First up, technical.

59
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.500
Like what processes, materials, or
software are we using?

60
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.500
Then, social.

61
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.500
How is this going to impact people and
their day-to-day relationships?

62
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.000
Next is procurement.

63
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Are we leasing, buying, building it
in-house, or maybe partnering up?

64
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.500
Then, management.

65
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.500
What do the life cycle and governance
actually look like?

66
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.500
And finally, transition.

67
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.039
Are we rolling the solution out in
carefully phased stages, or are we just

68
00:03:12.039 --> 00:03:14.000
going for a big bang release all at once?

69
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.550
Now, what's really interesting about this
slide is that your evaluation checklist

70
00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.500
doesn't just stop at those five lenses.

71
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.091
When you're looking at your requirements,
you also have to constantly weigh the

72
00:03:24.091 --> 00:03:25.500
value of alternative solutions.

73
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.500
You got to ask yourself, what's the
overall market capability right now?

74
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.804
Can the supply chain even deliver the
materials or services we need for this

75
00:03:32.804 --> 00:03:33.500
specific option?

76
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.654
Because let's be real, a solution that
looks great on paper is totally useless if

77
00:03:37.654 --> 00:03:39.500
the market can't actually supply it.

78
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.969
All right, section three, the art of
prioritizing, or what to do when you

79
00:03:44.969 --> 00:03:46.500
simply can't have it all.

80
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.500
And this brings us to a somewhat harsh,
but completely necessary reality.

81
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:56.700
Projects rarely, and I mean rarely, have
the budget, time, or scope to deliver

82
00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.500
absolutely everything that everyone wants.

83
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.500
So, ruthless prioritization becomes our
next critical filter.

84
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.500
One of the absolute best ways to do this
is the MoSCoW approach.

85
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.500
That stands for must have, should have,
could have, and won't have.

86
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Let's pull this back to AO's renovation
list for snappy appy.

87
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.222
Those sit/stand desks for the customer
service team, those align with strict

88
00:04:19.222 --> 00:04:23.500
occupational health and safety guidelines,
that makes them a legal must have.

89
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.500
But that beautiful communal green wall,
sure.

90
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.023
It's fantastic for well-being, but if the
budget gets tight or those historic

91
00:04:30.023 --> 00:04:33.500
building regulations push back, that green
wall firmly becomes a could have.

92
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.449
Sorting your requirements like this
ensures that the absolute essentials form

93
00:04:37.449 --> 00:04:39.500
the foundation of your optimal solution.

94
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.500
But MoSCoW isn't the only tool in your
tool belts.

95
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.500
You can also prioritize using these three
methods.

96
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.835
First, there's an investment appraisal
where you really dig into the trade-offs

97
00:04:49.835 --> 00:04:53.951
between costs, benefits, and risks to see
what truly adds value to the main

98
00:04:53.951 --> 00:04:54.500
objective.

99
00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.761
Second is the ABC model, which is just a
super simple way of classifying items

100
00:04:58.761 --> 00:05:01.000
purely based on their tier of importance.

101
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.500
And third, my personal favorite, the
brilliantly simple five whys method.

102
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.510
This is basically where you play the role
of a curious toddler, you just repeatedly

103
00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.375
ask stakeholders why a requirement is
actually necessary until you drill all the

104
00:05:13.375 --> 00:05:15.500
way down to the absolute root
justification.

105
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.196
Which brings us to section four,
delivering the optimal solution and

106
00:05:20.196 --> 00:05:21.500
securing the right path.

107
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.500
So, we've defined the problem, evaluated
our options, and prioritized the needs.

108
00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.891
Now it's time to select that single most
optimal solution and lock it into the

109
00:05:29.891 --> 00:05:30.500
business case.

110
00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.477
But hey, before we declare victory, we
really need to highlight a

111
00:05:34.477 --> 00:05:37.500
counter-intuitive but absolutely vital
rule in project management.

112
00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.500
The business case must always include the
option to do nothing.

113
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:41.500
Seriously.

114
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.237
Why? Because sometimes after all that
evaluation and prioritizing, you realize

115
00:05:45.237 --> 00:05:49.165
the cost or risk of actually implementing
a solution is way worse than the problem

116
00:05:49.165 --> 00:05:49.500
itself.

117
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.244
Sometimes the most strategic optimal
decision you can possibly make is to just

118
00:05:54.244 --> 00:05:56.500
walk away and leave things exactly as they
are.

119
00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.500
So, the crucial point is that you have to
watch out for stakeholder pressure.

120
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:01.500
It's a huge trap.

121
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.791
You are absolutely going to face powerful
individuals who are emotionally attached

122
00:06:05.791 --> 00:06:10.029
to their own preferred ideas and they'll
push for them, even when they aren't the

123
00:06:10.029 --> 00:06:10.500
best fit.

124
00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.500
But remember, your job isn't just to give
them what they want.

125
00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:17.230
Your job is to rely on that rigorous
evaluation funnel we just talked about,

126
00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:21.009
stick to the hard data, and protect the
project from those non-optimal vanity

127
00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:21.500
solutions.

128
00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.709
And finally, section five, solutions and
different life cycles, adapting to the

129
00:06:27.709 --> 00:06:28.500
environment.

130
00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:33.167
Okay, now that we have our optimal
solution on paper, how do we actually roll

131
00:06:33.167 --> 00:06:33.500
it out?

132
00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:36.500
Well, this shapes our whole approach to
execution.

133
00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.500
Let's look at the left side first, the
linear life cycle.

134
00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.500
In a linear project, you do all your
analysis right up front.

135
00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:46.626
You pick your optimal solution, you lock
in a strictly agreed scope, and from that

136
00:06:46.626 --> 00:06:50.500
point on, any tweaks have to go through a
super rigid change control process.

137
00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:52.500
It is a straight line.

138
00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.500
Now on the right side, we have the
iterative life cycle.

139
00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:56.500
This approach is way more fluid.

140
00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:00.500
It gives you the chance to explore
different design options for much longer.

141
00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.061
You investigate needs in focused time
boxes, and you deliver the solution

142
00:07:04.061 --> 00:07:06.500
incrementally, piece by piece, adapting as
you go.

143
00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:11.500
And this brilliantly illustrates how that
iterative cycle works in practice.

144
00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.100
Imagine you're managing a project to
launch a mobile app for a fast food

145
00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:16.500
company.

146
00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:20.932
A rival has just dropped a hugely popular
app and you're under intense pressure to

147
00:07:20.932 --> 00:07:22.500
get to market like yesterday.

148
00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:26.500
In an iterative cycle, you do not wait
around to build the perfect app.

149
00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:30.500
Phase one is launching a minimum viable
product or MVP.

150
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.985
You take only your absolute must have
requirements, maybe just viewing the menu

151
00:07:33.985 --> 00:07:36.500
and placing a basic order, and you get it
out there fast.

152
00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.500
Phase two then lets you gather progressive
user feedback from real customers.

153
00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:45.379
That feedback directly informs phase
three, where you refine the app into a

154
00:07:45.379 --> 00:07:49.517
minimum marketable product or MMP, which
is robust enough to actually compete in

155
00:07:49.517 --> 00:07:50.500
the broader market.

156
00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:54.935
Finally, in phase four and beyond, you
keep releasing future versions until all

157
00:07:54.935 --> 00:07:56.500
those original requirements are met.

158
00:07:56.500 --> 00:08:00.500
It's all about speed to value and learning
while you build.

159
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:03.500
Wow, we've covered a massive amount of
ground today.

160
00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:07.971
From defining chaotic problems and dodging
that space pen trap, all the way to

161
00:08:07.971 --> 00:08:12.500
evaluating requirements and rolling out
MVPs and MMPs in iterative life cycles.

162
00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:16.951
You now know exactly how to evaluate,
prioritize, and deliver the optimal

163
00:08:16.951 --> 00:08:17.500
solution.

164
00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.250
So, I want to leave you with a bit of a
provocative question to take into your

165
00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:22.500
very next project meeting.

166
00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:26.389
Next time a powerful stakeholder demands
their favorite non-optimal preferred

167
00:08:26.389 --> 00:08:30.126
solution, exactly which lenses and
criteria are you going to use to filter

168
00:08:30.126 --> 00:08:32.500
their request and guide them to the right
path?

169
00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.500
Keep that funnel in mind, stay focused,
and keep learning.

170
00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:37.500
Catch you next time.
